The recompiler's guest interrupt lock keeps each thread's interrupt state in that thread. Every mfmsr took and released
the global critical region only to read a process-wide count, and every mtmsrd r13 section changed that count with
two atomic read-modify-writes. mfmsr now reads the calling thread's own depth without a lock and returns the same
value (0x8000 outside a section, 0 inside), while mtmsrd r13 still enters the global critical region and the next
mtmsrd still leaves it, so the exclusion against other guest threads and host code holding the region is unchanged.
The generated code calls rexruntime for all three, so every module is generated and compiled again.

diff --git a/include/rex/thread/mutex.h b/include/rex/thread/mutex.h
--- a/include/rex/thread/mutex.h
+++ b/include/rex/thread/mutex.h
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
 
 #pragma once
 
+#include <cstdint>
 #include <mutex>
 
 namespace rex::thread {
@@ -82,4 +83,10 @@ class global_critical_region {
   }
 };
 
+// Recompiled code disables guest interrupts (mtmsrd r13) by entering the global critical region and enables them by
+// leaving it. Only the calling thread changes its own interrupt state, so reading it (mfmsr) takes no lock.
+uint64_t GuestInterruptMsr();
+void EnterGuestInterruptLock();
+void LeaveGuestInterruptLock();
+
 }  // namespace rex::thread
